# Xcode Build & Deploy

When asked to **build**, **rebuild**, or **deploy** the app to a device:

- Stay focused on completing the requested build/deployment. Do not fix unrelated issues or refactor code.
- First attempt the build/run using the existing Xcode project, scheme, and device configuration. Do not proactively **clean**, change settings, reinstall dependencies, or modify signing.
- If the build **succeeds**, stop. Report success and any meaningful warnings. Do not fix warnings unless asked.
- If the build **fails**, identify the most likely root error and make only a **targeted** fix directly related to that error.
- After a fix, rebuild once. If it still fails, make at most **one additional** targeted fix and rebuild.
- Never perform more than **2 fix/rebuild cycles** in one request. If the build still fails, stop and report the error rather than continuing to troubleshoot.
- Do not chase cascading or unrelated errors. Focus on the earliest/root failure.
- Keep build output focused: inspect relevant errors/warnings rather than repeatedly dumping large Xcode logs into context.
- Once the requested build/deployment succeeds, **stop immediately**.

## Overrides (stricter workflows)

| User asks for | Follow instead |
|---|---|
| **Commit / push / ship** | **`xcode-run-test.mdc`** preflight (**`clean`** → **`clean build-for-testing`** → **`test`**); fix errors, failures, and project warnings per **`xcode-fix-build-warnings.mdc`** until green — iteration cap does not apply. |
| **Fix warnings** (explicit) | **`xcode-fix-build-warnings.mdc`** for that request. |
| **Run on Dre's Phone** / device launch | **`xcode-run-dres-phone.mdc`** for destination, install, and launch steps; build behavior still follows this rule unless commit/push preflight applies. |
